      "role": "user",
      "content": "Thanks for those. The \"Standard Professional\" one is perfect. The \"Casual/Urban\" one is a good start, but I'm worried it might still feel a bit too \"polished\" or like \"corporate trying to be cool\" for those specific inner-city demographics. \n\nWe want to maximize that rapport. Could you refine that second template to be even more authentic? Like, lean more into the actual vernacular and slang that you'd hear in those neighborhoods so the customer feels like they're talking to someone who actually understands their vibe and isn't just some suit reading a script.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "You're right,\"Corporate trying to be cool\" often sounds insincere, so let's make the \"Casual/Urban\" template feel raw and genuine without relying on stereotypes. Here is a more authentic version that uses natural phrasing and a direct, friendly voice:\n\n**Authentic Casual/Urban Template:**\n\"Yo, appreciate you letting us know about that delay. We hear you, nobody likes a hold-up on their stuff, so we're making sure this gets moved out of here ASAP at no cost to ya. Expect it at your door by [New Date], and if it ain't on the way, just hit us up straight up. We're on it, no stress.\"\n\nThis version drops the scripted甜味άζ (virtual sugar) for a grittier, conversational tone that acknowledges shared frustrations while offering a direct, low-friction solution. However, please remember that authentic communication still requires deep cultural research and ongoing feedback loops, as every neighborhood has a unique dialect.",
